  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nHere in southern Appalachia, we're getting another bout of homegrown \nshowers and thunderstorms today. A diving trough cutting to the \nsoutheast underneath a Rex block will combine a good vort max, some \nmild upper divergence, and warm and moist air to produce another \nround of scattered to numerous thunderstorms later today. Effective \nshear is present underneath all that, around 20 knots, deep layer \nshear a little stronger, so depending on the storms it's possible \nthere's one or two severe storms for wind gusts, though I'm not as \nsold on a severe hail risk. Otherwise storm motions are forecast to \nbe near 10 knots, so slow moving storms do present a risk for flash \nflooding, especially if on previously saturated soils or vulnerable \nsoils. Both 00z REFS and 00z HREF depict heavy rain swaths enough to \ninduce flash flooding in the mountain counties south of Knoxville \nfor today, but I wouldn't take the exact locations as gospel. \nThursday skinny and somewhat shallow vertical profiles will still \nsupport a few showers, with perhaps more scattered light rain under \nthe still moist atmosphere.\n\nChattanooga. Congratulations. After 50 days of 90F or hotter \nweather, you have attained the next part of the year: still warm but \nnot excruciatingly hot. Possibly even more shocking are the low \ntemperatures, after 100+ days of minimums being 60F or greater, \nChattanooga will hit the 50s beginning this weekend. \n\nGFS/Euro are in agreement that we'll be on the backside of a solid \nnor'easter on the Eastern seaboard this weekend, which means we'll \nbe on the receiving end of dry continental air while that storm \nsystem matures off the New England coast. Might have a few \noverperforming radiation nights this weekend in Virginia, skies look \nto be clear in the low PWAT environment with very dry air present. \nIt is conceivable that a few colder hollows might get into, gasp, \nthe upper 30s Saturday and Sunday mornings! First taste of fall has \narrived. Daytime afternoon RH values are likely to lead to a very \npleasant weekend, which is the best benefit from dry air plus still \nmildly warm temperatures.",
